  "markdown": {
    "instructions": [
      "This is the documentation of the Darvas data API and of custom indicators for Darvas charts.",
      "Every page is listed with a summary in https://docs.darvas.app/llms.txt; any page is Markdown at its URL with .md added. The OpenAPI spec of every endpoint is https://docs.darvas.app/data-api/openapi.yaml.",
      "Call the API at https://darvas.app/api/data/v1 with the API key in the X-API-KEY header. Name a company by ticker or by CIK; dates are YYYY-MM-DD.",
      "A list answer holds one page; request its next_page_url as it is until it has none. limit counts rows across all pages.",
      "Errors are JSON with an error code and a message. A 429 carries Retry-After, the seconds to wait; each key has its own requests per minute.",
      "The same endpoints are tools at the MCP server https://darvas.app/api/data/v1/mcp, with the same key. https://docs.darvas.app/data-api/ai-agents explains every way an agent can read these docs.",
      "Custom indicators for Darvas charts are Pine Script v5 or v6 indicator() or strategy() scripts. Start at https://docs.darvas.app/introduction. https://docs.darvas.app/pine/support lists what Darvas runs and https://docs.darvas.app/pine/other-data the data request.security() and request.financial() read.",
      "Pages whose summary starts with \"Darvas JavaScript\" describe a second runtime that the Darvas editor does not offer. Write new indicators in Pine Script.",
      "Darvas Pro+ webhooks send a signed POST for alarm fires and pauses, scheduled answers, economic calendar alerts, ETF flow updates, ended connections and the data API events. https://docs.darvas.app/pro/webhooks lists every event, its object and how to verify the Darvas-Signature header."
    ]
  },
